        '9-12', $q$Reading the Climate in Ancient Ice$q$, $q$(1) In the interior of Greenland and Antarctica, snow has fallen year after year for hundreds of thousands of years without ever fully melting. (2) As each new layer settles on top of the last, its weight slowly compresses the snow beneath it into dense glacial ice. (3) Tiny bubbles of air become sealed inside that ice during the process, preserving samples of the atmosphere as it existed when the snow first fell. (4) ____, a cylinder of ice drilled from deep within a glacier works something like a frozen archive, with the oldest records stored at the bottom.

(5) Extracting those records requires patience as well as specialized equipment. (6) Drilling teams lower a hollow drill through the ice in short runs, lifting out sections a few meters long and labeling each one with its exact depth. (7) The sections are then shipped in freezers to laboratories, where researchers slice them into thin samples. (8) Some polar research stations also host visiting artists who paint the surrounding landscape. (9) In the laboratory, scientists measure the gases trapped in the bubbles and the chemical makeup of the ice itself.

(10) The results have reshaped scientists' understanding of how the climate changes over long periods. (11) By comparing the amount of carbon dioxide in each layer with evidence of past temperatures, researchers have shown that the two have risen and fallen together across many ice ages. (12) Layers of volcanic ash, ____, allow scientists to match specific bands of ice to eruptions recorded in history, which helps confirm the age of the core. (13) Ice cores, in short, let researchers read a record that no human witness could have kept.$q$, 'draft')
