   $q$Read the sentences: "The bridge opened in 1932. It was made of steel. It spanned the river gorge." Which revision combines them most effectively?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The bridge, which spanned the river gorge, opened in 1932.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"drops-detail"},{"text":"The steel bridge, which spanned the river gorge, opened in 1932.","is_correct":true},{"text":"The bridge opened in 1932, it was made of steel, it spanned the river gorge.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"comma-splice"},{"text":"The bridge opened in 1932 and it was made of steel and it spanned the river gorge.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"stringy-coordination"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The steel bridge, which spanned the river gorge, opened in 1932.$q$, $q$Turning "made of steel" into the adjective "steel" and the gorge detail into a clause keeps all three facts in one smooth sentence. The choice "The bridge, which spanned the river gorge, opened in 1932" is smooth too, but it drops the fact that the bridge was steel.$q$, 'draft', null)
