   $q$Which revision of sentence 12 best concludes the draft while staying accurate to all of the evidence, including sentence 11?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Oyster reefs are better than seawalls in every way, so every coastal town should build them right away.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overgeneralization"},{"text":"Because one reef failed in a storm, Okafor's experiment shows that reefs are too unreliable to depend on.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"overweights-exception"},{"text":"Seawalls and oyster reefs both have strengths and weaknesses, so neither one is truly better than the other.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"false-balance"},{"text":"Where the water is calm enough for oysters to settle, Okafor's results show reefs offer a stronger, self-repairing defense.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Where the water is calm enough for oysters to settle, Okafor's results show reefs offer a stronger, self-repairing defense.$q$, $q$The correct conclusion keeps the main claim but limits it to calm water, which is exactly the condition sentence 11 sets. The "every way" version ignores the storm failure, while the "too unreliable" version lets one failed site outweigh six years of results.$q$, 'draft', 'aa188600-a618-5070-b6ce-b986ea6b4591')
